The public blog you are looking at is built with the [platformOS design system](https://documentation.platformos.com) — the `common-styling` module. Every color, font, spacing and component on this page comes from a shared set of design tokens, which means your blog stays consistent with the rest of your platformOS application out of the box.

# One design system, light and dark

The design system defines its palette as CSS custom properties with light and dark variants. The blog enables automatic theme switching, so if your operating system prefers a dark color scheme, this page is already rendering in dark mode — no configuration required.

# Components, not snowflakes

The building blocks you see around this text are reusable components:

- **Cards** display the posts on the home page, the author box and the previous/next navigation.
- **Tags** power the category filter and the topic labels on every post.
- **Buttons** in the header and the pagination all share the same sizing and states.
- **Typography** for this very article is handled by the prose styles, tuned for comfortable reading.

# Make it yours

Because everything is driven by design tokens, rebranding the blog usually means overriding a handful of CSS custom properties — an accent color here, a heading font there — instead of rewriting stylesheets. Head over to the style guide page of the design system to see every component and token in one place.
